\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 2: Abstract choice and preference theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Choice theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section, we introduce choice and revealed preference theory.
 In the 
\series bold
choice
\series default
 
\series bold
model
\series default
 an agent is represented by his/her choices in the possible choice problems
 that he/she may face.
 Our primitive in the choice model is also a set of alternatives 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Mathematical aside: power set and correspondences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(21).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 be a set.
 The 
\series bold
power set of A
\series default
, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{A}$
\end_inset

, is the set of all subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 For each element 
\begin_inset Formula $B\in2^{A}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq A$
\end_inset

.
 Using this notation, the set of non-empty sets of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{A}\backslash\{\emptyset\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Technically, a correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $C:A\rightrightarrows B$
\end_inset

 is a function 
\begin_inset Formula $C:A\rightarrow2^{B}$
\end_inset

.
 An alternative notation for 
\begin_inset Formula $C:A\rightrightarrows B$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $C:A\rightsquigarrow B$
\end_inset

.
 The definition of a correspondence should specifiy its name, domain, co-domain,
 and the image of each element in its domain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(22).

\series default
 
\emph on
A 
\series bold
correspondence
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

, from a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a mathematical object that associates with each 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A$
\end_inset

 a subset 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $C(a)\subseteq B$
\end_inset

.
 The generic correspondence from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $C:A\rightrightarrows B$
\end_inset

.
 Correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
non-empty valued
\series default
 if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C(a)\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Choice structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(23).
 
\series default
\emph on
A 
\series bold
choice structure
\series default
 is a pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

 is a non-empty family of non-empty subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}\subseteq2^{X}\backslash\{\emptyset\},\ B\in\mathcal{B}\implies B\subseteq X$
\end_inset

) and 
\begin_inset Formula $C:\mathcal{B}\rightrightarrows X$
\end_inset

 is a non-empty valued correspondence such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $B\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B)\subseteq B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We interpret 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

 as a family of possible choice problems that the agent may face.
 For each choice problem 
\begin_inset Formula $B\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

, the agent selects 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B)$
\end_inset

 as his/her best choices.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B)$
\end_inset

 contains a single element, that element is the individual's choice form
 among the alternatives in 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 The set 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B)$
\end_inset

 may, however, contain more than one element.
 When it does, the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B)$
\end_inset

 are the alternatives in 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 that the decision maker 
\emph on
might
\emph default
 choose; that is, they are her 
\emph on
acceptable alternatives
\emph default
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 In this case, the set 
\begin_inset Formula $C(B)$
\end_inset

 can be thought of as containing those alternatives that we would actually
 see chosen if the decision maker were repeatedly to face the problem of
 choosing an alternative from set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the same spirit of rationality of preferences, one may be interested
 in studying choice structures that satisfy certain consistency properties.
 We may want to impose some 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reasonable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 restrictions regarding an individual's choice behavior.
 The following property has played a central role in the literature.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series bold
Intuition:
\series default
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 be a choice structure, 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

 a choice problem, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(A)$
\end_inset

.
 This 
\bar under
reveals
\bar default
 that the agent favors the choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 over all the other elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

 and suppose that in another choice problem, say 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, both 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 are available and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is chosen at 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 It is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reasonable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to think that the agent will also choose 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 since at choice problem 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 the agent revealed being in favor of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 makes it to the top, so does 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(24).
 
\series default
\emph on
A choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the 
\series bold
Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference (WARP) 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{A,B\}\subseteq\mathcal{B},\ \forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq A\cap B:\left[x\in C(A)\ \wedge\ y\in C(B)\right]\implies x\in C(B)$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When the family of choice problems 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

 is rich enough, WARP may be divided into two independent properties.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first property is interpreted as: 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The champion of the world is the champion of his/her own country.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(25).
 
\series default
\emph on
A choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies 
\series bold
Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset


\series default
\emph default
 
\emph on
iff
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{A,B\}\subseteq\mathcal{B},\ \forall\ x\in C(B)\cap A:A\subseteq B\implies x\in C(A)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The second property is interpreted as: 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

If the champion of a country is the champion of the world, then all champions
 of that country are champions of the world.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(26).
 
\series default
\emph on
A choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies 
\series bold
Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset


\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{A,B\}\subseteq\mathcal{B},\ \forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq C(A):\left[A\subseteq B\ \wedge\ x\in C(B)\right]\implies y\in C(B)$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
WARP implies Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Though, in general, Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 do not imply WARP.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ B\in\mathcal{B},\ \forall\ \emptyset\neq B^{\prime}\subseteq B,\ B^{\prime}\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

, then WARP is equivalent to Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and Sen's property 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\emph on
(3).
 
\series default
\emph default
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 be a choice structure such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $B\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\emptyset\neq B^{\prime}\subseteq B,B^{\prime}\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP if and only if it satisfies both Sen's properties 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 be a choice structure such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $B\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\emptyset\neq B^{\prime}\subseteq B,B^{\prime}\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP if and only if it satisfies both Sen's properties 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP.
 We prove that it satisfies Sen's 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Property 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq B\subseteq X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(B)\cap A$
\end_inset

.
 We want to prove 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(A)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 is non-empty valued, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C(A)\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in C(A)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq B$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq A\cap B,x\in C(B),y\in C(A)\implies x\in C(A)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Property 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq B\subseteq X,\ \{x,y\}\subseteq C(A),\ x\in C(B)$
\end_inset

.
 We want to prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in C(B)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $C(A)\subseteq A\subseteq B$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq A\cap B$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq A\cap B,x\in C(B),y\in C(A)\implies y\in C(B)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Sen's 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 We prove that it satisfies WARP.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 be two non-empty subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq A\cap B$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(A)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in C(B)$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(B).$
\end_inset

 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $D\equiv A\cap B$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq A\cap B$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $D\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 By hypothesis, 
\begin_inset Formula $D\in\mathcal{B}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\emptyset\neq C(D)\subseteq D$
\end_inset

.
 Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Sen's 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in C(B)\cap D$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D\subseteq B$
\end_inset

 imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in C(D)$
\end_inset

.
 By the same argument, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(A)\cap D$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D\subseteq A$
\end_inset

 imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(D)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B},C)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Sen's 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq C(D)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D\subseteq B$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in C(B)$
\end_inset

 imply that 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $x\in C(B)$
\end_inset


